{
  "rationale": "Evidence from the last race (iteration 2, score 132.0): 59 of 60 development trials ended off track, and only one oval trial finished. Progress was 0.45 on the oval, 0.27 on the s-bend and 0.16 on the slalom. Max error sat at the 0.06 m off-track limit in every trial, and RMS error was a steady 0.03 m. Line loss fell from 1.0 to 0.65 per s-bend trial after widening the sensor span to ±0.06 m and enlarging the footprint to 16 mm, so the span change helped. Steering oscillation rose to 0.22 to 0.27 when kp went from 2.0 to 3.0. Iteration 1 showed that lower speed plus more damping did not help, and line loss was zero there while the robot still drifted off, so the failure is gradual drift on curves rather than sudden loss of the tape. A steady 0.03 m RMS offset with no line loss points to a constant bias. The fixed left/right motor mismatch in the disturbance is the likely source: a proportional-only controller settles with a steady offset, and the integral term can cancel it. The off-track limit is 0.06 m for every allowed body width, so body width cannot help.\n\nChanges: (1) Keep the iteration-2 geometry (5 sensors at y=0.05, x from -0.06 to +0.06, 16 mm footprint), since it gave the only gain in line loss and the only finish. (2) Add a small integral gain, ki 0.5, to cancel the constant motor-mismatch bias that shows up as steady RMS error. This is well inside the integral limit of ±2 and the gain range. (3) Raise kd from 0.4 to 0.6 to damp the extra oscillation that kp 3.0 produced. (4) Keep kp 3.0 and base_speed 1.3, so the only changes are the bias correction and the damping, which keeps the effect of each change readable.\n\nExpected result: RMS error should fall below about 0.02 m and max error should drop below 0.06 m on the s-bend and slalom. Line-loss events should fall toward zero, and progress should rise above 0.35 on the development tracks. Completion within 3 s is still uncertain, because the robot must average about 1.5 m/s.",
